negotiators at un's cop 27 climate  Summit in Egypt have approved an  historic deal on compensation for  vulnerable Nations after days of  gridlock countries at the summit agreed  to set up a loss and damage fund to help  the nation's most at risk of climate  change disasters calls for such a fund  have dominated the last two weeks of  talks the details of the deal still have  to be negotiated including which  countries would foot the bill now while  the deal has won praise many officials  say it falls short on both loss and  damaged provisions and on commitment to  reducing emissions here's what eu's  climate policy Chief Franz timmermans  had to say we are proud of our  contribution to the creation of this  fund that will serve the interest of  those most in need of support but I urge  you to acknowledge when you walk out of  this room that we have all fallen short  in actions to avoid and minimize loss  and damage we should have done much more  our citizens expect us to lead that  means  far more rapidly reduce emissions  DW climate reporter Ajit naranjan joins  me now Ajit so they've agreed on a deal  where richer countries will pay poorer  countries will help them out to uh to  deal with the damage climate change has  done what does this mean in Practical  terms  it means that rich countries are now  forced to not only look at helping poor  countries adapt so building higher sea  walls and things like that or clean up  their energy grids and kind of helping  them build renewable energy and wind  turbines and solar panels and so on but  also that they're accepting some of the  financial responsibility for extreme  weather events that have been made worse  by the fossil fuels we've burnt  disproportionately in countries like  Germany and the US in France and so on  and so this is a huge step forward  because for 30 years or so poorer  countries have kind of been asking for  this and all these climate talks it's  never been something that's even been on  the agenda this year for the first time  it got put onto the discussion table and  after kind of huge two weeks of  incredibly intense negotiations  countries like economies like the EU and  the US which have previously blocked all  attempts at this have relented to some  degree and said they'll set up a fund  so it's a big step forward but there are  those who say that this fund already  falls short of what is needed  exactly I mean there's two ways you can  look at it the first is that rich  countries definitely do not want to  accept any liability for the damages  done because they feel that if they I  mean one of the big barriers to them  setting up the fund in the first place  with this feeling that if they agree to  any type of financial responsibility  then they'll be left with a bill that'll  run into the trillions of dollars  the second aspect of this is that  they've repeatedly failed to keep their  promises so in the climate Summit in  2009 in Copenhagen rich countries agreed  to pay poor countries a hundred billion  dollars a year by 2020. that money never  materialized it got to about 83 billion  by 2020 and even now today they're still  trying to catch up and get to that 100  billion numbers so I think there's a lot  of skepticism from the poorest countries  that the amount of money needed will be  even put in the spot  okay so apart from the fund what else is  in the deal that's been agreed in  Channel check and more importantly  what's not in the deal so there's a lot  of technicalities in which there's been  progress uh on all sorts of areas but  the key thing that's missing really from  here is very clear statements about  getting rid of fossil fuels so the only  line that the deal contains referring to  them is saying getting rid of unabated  unabated coal power which means coal  power plants that haven't been fitted  with technology to capture the carbon  dioxide from them and phasing out  inefficient fossil fuel subsidies  there's no clear definition on what  unabated actually means in this context  how good these carbon capture  Technologies need to be and there's no  clear definition on what inefficient  means when you're subsidizing fossil  fuel projects what's really absent from  this document that we saw this morning  is words like oil and gas which are  fossil fuels that when you burn them  heat the planet and we know from the  science the science reports that this  same declaration welcomed explicitly  they make it very clear we need to  rapidly get rid of these fossil fuel  sources there's still room in the mix  for a small amount by 2050. but then  with the Technologies to capture them in  place and definitely on a scale that's  drastically lower than what we've got  now right so a mixed result then in  terms of climate policy in Egypt thank  you very much uh DW climate reporter
 

